  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1216 AM EST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nPleasant fall weather can be expected over the next few days as a \nlarge area of surface high pressure remains over the region. Expect \nchilly nights and mild days with high temperatures in the 60s for \nmost valley locations each day through Wednesday.   \n\nA change will be arriving for the latter part of the week as models \ncontinue to show a shift to more winter-like conditions.  While \nthere are disagreements in the details, models generally show an \nupper low digging south out of Canada Thursday and Friday as \nmultiple short waves rotate around the upper low carving out a deep \neastern CONUS trough. \n\nAn initial short wave and associated cold front will likely bring \nsome rain to the area Wednesday night into Thursday. Colder air will \nmove in behind the front, and the colder air will then be reinforced \nas subsequent short waves rotate around the upper low. The details \nare still uncertain this far out and will be important, but this set \nup looks favorable for some wintry precipitation especially across \nthe higher elevations, with accumulating mountain snowfall possible \nthat will likely be enhanced by upslope flow. High temperatures may \nstruggle to make it much above the 40s in many valley locations both \nFriday and Saturday.",
